## ABSTRACT
Porous liquids (PLs), an emerging porous material with permanent cavities, have attracted extensive attention in recent years. However, the current construction methods are complicated and resulting PLs possess high viscosity values, which cannot meet the requirements of practical industrial applications. Herein, we demonstrate a generalizable and simple strategy to prepare type III  PLs  with  low  viscosity  based  on  the  rule  of  “like  dissolves  like”.  Specifically,  the  monoglycidyl  ether  terminated polydimethylsiloxane (denoted by E-PDMS) is attached to the surface of Universitetet i Oslo (UiO)- $.66\substack{-N H_{2}}$ via covalent linkage, constructing the pore generator $(\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -E-PDMS, denoted by P-UiO-66). Then, P-UiO-66 is dispersed into different types and amounts of sterically hindered solvents (PDMS400 or PDMS6000), obtaining a series of type III PLs (denoted by P-UiO-66- PLs) with permanent cavities and low viscosities. The gas sorption–desorption test shows that P-UiO-66-PLs have an enormous potential  for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selective  separation.  Besides,  the  porosity  of  P-UiO-66-PLs  and  the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ sorption  mechanism  are demonstrated  by  molecular  simulation.  Furthermore,  the  generality  of  the  synthesis  strategy  is  confirmed  by  the  successful construction  of  PLs  using  two  other  amino-metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs) $(M I L-53(A I)-N H_{2}$ and $\mathsf{M I L-88B}(\mathsf{F e})\ –\mathsf{N H_{2}})$ . Importantly, it’s worth noting that the strategy based on the rule of “like dissolves like” sheds light on the preparation of other types of PLs for task-specific applications.
## KEYWORDS
porous liquids, like dissolves like, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture, low viscosity
## Introduction
The  sharply  rising  levels  of  anthropogenic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emission  have resulted in serious linkage problems including global warming and climate change, which may finally threaten the human survival. In this  context,  the “Paris  Agreement” was  proposed  and  widely approved  for  the  final  goals  of  carbon  neutrality  and  peak $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emissions [1–3]. Carbon capture, utilization, and storage (CCUS) has  been  widely  considered  one  of  the  effective  technologies  to achieve the goal, which mainly lies in the development of efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture technologies and materials [4–6].
Porous solid materials (e.g., metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), porous  coordination  polymers  (PCPs),  etc.)  are  deemed  as promising  materials  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture  due  to  their  advantages including  inherent  pore  structures,  high  adsorption  capacity, decent  material  stability,  etc.  [7–14].  Anyhow,  porous  solid materials  are  difficult  to  be  used  in  the  current  mostly  used flowing-related pump process. By contrast, liquid absorbents can be  easily  connected  to  the  flowing-related  pump  [15–17]. However,  the  volatilization  and  low  selectivity  issues  of  liquid absorbents are also manifested [18].
Toward  the  above  issues,  porous  liquids  (PLs),  an  emerging class of liquid materials with permanent cavities, are first proposed by  James  and  co-workers  in  2007  [19],  but  not  synthesized. Undoubtedly,  PLs  have  garnered  substantial  attention  since  the concept was proposed. PLs integrate beneficial features of porous solid materials and liquid materials, exhibiting great promises in various applications, such as gas sorption and separation [20–27], catalysis [28, 29], etc. According to the component functions, PLs can  be  considered  as  two  parts:  pore  generators  and  sterically hindered solvents. Till now, a solid body of studies on PLs have been  published  due  to  the  rich  library  of  pore  generators  and sterically  hindered  solvents.  Meanwhile,  various  strategies  for constructing PLs have been reported especially during the past five years [30–33]. For example, in 2015, Dai et al. reported a surface engineering  method  with  suitable  corona  and  canopy  species  to construct PLs based hollow silica (HS) and favorable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selective sorption  and  separation  performance  were  obtained  [34]. Similarly,  our  research  group  has  adopted  the  ionically  or covalently tethered corona-canopy species to prepare MOF-based PLs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture [24, 25, 35]. In another study, our group and Dai  et  al.  demonstrated  an  electrostatic-assisted  liquefaction method to prepare hollow carbon (HC) spheres-based PLs with superior gas sorption performance [36]. More recently, we have reported an in-situ coupling strategy to fabricate PLs based on HC nanospheres  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption  [37].  Overall,  several  approaches have been demonstrated to prepare PLs for superior gas sorption performances. However, previously reported preparation methods are  relatively  complicated.  For  example,  the  formation  of polymerized ionic liquids layers is a requisite for the following redispersion  process  by  the in-situ coupling  strategy.  Besides,  the viscosities  of  reported  PLs  are  relatively  high,  hindering  further utilization in the practical flowing process [38, 39]. In addition, the used synthesis materials are relatively not cost-effective, hindering the further application in practical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture processes. Thus, these  challenges  highlight  that  a  facile  and  feasible  strategy  to construct  PLs  with  low  viscosity  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture  is  urgently required.
According  to  an  army  of  previous  studies,  the  postmodification of porous solid materials to obtain pore generators is of significance for the creation of permanent porosity in PLs. The effects  of  the  post-modification  profile  can  be  attributed  to  the following  two  aspects.  Firstly,  it  contributes  to  the  dispersion  of pore  generators  in  sterically  hindered  solvents.  Secondly,  the polymer chains are always utilized in the post-modification step, which  is  beneficial  to  permanent  porosity  retention.  Moreover, other articles have documented that the rule of “like dissolves like” can increase the dispersibility of nanoparticles in polymer matrices [26, 40, 41].  Considering  the  above  points,  it  can  be  expected  to construct PLs based on the rule of “like dissolves like”. However, as far as we know, there have been no studies on constructing PLs using the rule of “like dissolves like” till now.
(denoted by E-PDMS) was attached to the surface of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ via  the  covalent  linkage,  obtaining  the  pore  generator  (UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -E-PDMS, denoted by P-UiO-66) (Scheme S1 in the ESM). At last, a series of type III PLs (denoted by P-UiO-66-PLs) were obtained  based  on  the  rule  of “like  dissolves  like” by  dispersing various amounts of P-UiO-66 into the common and cost-effective PDMS  (Scheme  1(b)).  PDMS  was  chosen  as  the  sterically hindered solvent because it is a kind of colorless, transparent, nontoxic  and  tasteless  liquid  material,  possessing  the  advantages  of heat  resistance,  cold  resistance,  a  slight  change  of  viscosity  with temperature, water resistance, small surface tension, etc. And its excellent smooth effect as a sterically hindered solvent can provide low viscosity for porous liquids. As expected, all the P-UiO-66-PLs presented a superior flowing ability at ambient temperature and were  extremely  stable  after  setting  for  120  days.  Besides,  to confirm  the  universality  of  this  method,  PDMS  with  different molecular weights (PDMS400 or PDMS6000) was utilized as flow media to construct PLs. Finally, P-UiO-66-PLs were used for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture,  and  gas  sorption  behaviors  were  systematically investigated,  which  revealed  that  P-UiO-66-PLs  possessed  great potential  in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selective  sorption.  Moreover,  the  residual porosity of P-UiO-66-PLs was verified by diverse characterizations and  molecular  simulation,  and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selective  capture  sites  were determined  by  Grand  Canonical  Monte  Carlo  (GCMC).  More importantly,  the  proposed  strategy  based  on  the  rule  of “like dissolves like” could be extended to prepare other classes of PLs, considering the rich library of pore generators, such as covalent organic frameworks (COFs), metal organic polyhedra (MOP), etc., which will undoubtedly advance the further development of PLs toward practical gas capture applications.
## 2    Experimental
## 2.1    Materials
Zirconium(IV) chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4},}$ $98\%$ ) and 2-amino-1,4- benzenedicarboxylic  acid $(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2},$ $98\%$ )  were  provided  by Macklin.  Acetic  acid  glacial $(99.5\%)$ ,  N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ ),  acetone $(99.5\%)$ ,  and  toluene $(99.5\%)$ were provided by Guangdong Guanghua Sci-Tech $C0.$ ., Ltd. E-PDMS was  purchased  from  Sigma-Aldrich.  Polydimethylsiloxane, trimethylsiloxy  terminated $(M_{\mathrm{W}}\ 400$ and $M_{\mathrm{W}}~6000$ ,  denoted  as PDMS400 and PDMS6000) were purchased from Alfa Aesar. All the other chemicals were used without further purification.
## 2.2    Synthesis of $\mathbf{UiO-66-NH}_{2}$
$\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was synthesized with minor adjustments according to the reported methods [41, 42]. First, $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $\left(0.48\mathrm{g},2.058\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ was  added  into  DMF $(60~\mathrm{mL}$ )  and  sonicated  for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ until dissolved. Then, ${\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ $(372.81~\mathrm{mg},2.058~\mathrm{mmol},$ ) was added to the above solution and sonicated for 10 min. Next, acetic acid glacial (6 mL) was added to the solution. The mixture was loaded into a polytetrafluoroethylene hydrothermal reactor and reacted at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After the reaction solution was cooled to room temperature, it was washed three times with DMF and acetone at room temperature, and then centrifuged. At last, it was collected after drying in an oven.
The synthesis of MIL-53(Al)- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can be found in the ESM.
## 2.3    Synthesis of pore generator P-UiO-66
P-UiO-66  was  prepared  by  modifying $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ with  EPDMS.  First,  predesigned  amount  of  E-PDMS  was  added  to toluene $(20~\mathrm{mL})$ ) and sonicated until it was uniformly dispersed. Then, $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(0.2\ {\tt g})$ was added to the above solution and sonicated until dissolved. The solution was heated at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5{-}8\mathrm{h}$ and then dried to obtain the pore generator (modified UiO$66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}^{\cdot},$ ), and it was denoted by P-UiO-66.
## 2.4    Synthesis of porous liquids
The pore generator P-UiO-66 was dispersed in sterically hindered solvents PDMS400 and PDMS6000, respectively, based on the rule of “like dissolves like”, obtaining PLs. First, the as-prepared P-UiO66 (0.4 g) was added to toluene $\mathrm{20~mL}$ and sonicated for $10\mathrm{min}$ . Then PDMS400 (0.2, 0.4, 0.8, and ${1.6\ \mathrm{g}},$ respectively) was added into the above solution and stirred at room temperature for $5\mathrm{{h}}$ excess PDMS400 was removed by dialysis and four classes of PLs were obtained. And they are denoted by P-UiO-66-PL-X $\left(X=1,2,\right.$ 3,  4),  where $X$ represents  that  the  mass  ratio  of  P-UiO-66  to PDMS400 is 1:0.5, 1:1, 1:2, and 1:4, respectively. Similarly, P-UiO66-PL-Y $\mathit{\check{Y}}=5,6,7,8,$ was prepared by adding PDMS6000 (0.2, 0.4, 0.8, and $1.6\mathrm{g},$ respectively) to the dispersion solution of P-UiO66,  where $Y$ represents  that  the  mass  ratio  of  P-UiO-66  to PDMS6000 is 1:0.5, 1:1, 1:2, and 1:4, respectively.
PLs  based $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were  also prepared and denoted by P-MIL-53(Al)-PL- $P$ $\langle P=1,2\rangle$ ) and PMIL-88B(Fe)-PL-Q $Q=1,2$ ), where $P$ represents that the mass ratios of P-MIL-53(Al) to PDMS400 and PDMS6000 are both 1:1, and $Q$ represents  that  the  mass  ratios  of  P-MIL-88B(Fe)  to PDMS400 and PDMS6000 are both 1:1. The detailed preparation process can be found in the ESM.
## 2.5    Characterization methods
The morphological characteristics were observed using a scanning electron microscope (SEM, Verios G4, FEI) and a high-resolution transmission electron microscope (TEM, FEI Talos F200X). X-ray diffractometry  (XRD)  curves  were  collected  using  a  Bruker  D2 PHASER X diffractometer with $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\alpha$ radiation at a step size of $0.02^{\circ}$ . Nitrogen adsorption isotherms were measured at 77 K using a  Micromeritics  ASAP  2020  instrument.  Before  measurements, samples were degassed in a vacuum at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for at least $12\mathrm{{h}}$ . The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method was used to determine the specific surface areas at relative pressures in the range of $P/P_{0}=$
0.05–0.30.  Fourier  transform  infrared  (FTIR)  spectra  were obtained  on  a  Nicolet  7199  spectrometer.  Thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) were performed on a TGA Q50 TA instrument under $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere  (heating  rate  of $10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ ,  temperature range of $25\mathrm{-}700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . The surface elemental composition of UiO$66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and  P-UiO-66  was  measured  using  an  X-ray photoelectron  spectrometer  (XPS,  Kratos  Axis  Ultra  DLD).  The differential  scanning  calorimetry  (DSC)  curves  were  collected through a DSC Q100 TA instrument under $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere at a heating rate of $10\mathrm{^{\circ}C cdot m i n^{-1}}$ . Rheological properties were measured by  torque  rheometer  Haake  PolyLab  OS. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption–desorption behaviors of P-UiO-66-PLs were tested on a 3H-2000PH  instrument  (Beishide  Instrument  Technology (Beijing) $\mathrm{Co}$ .,  Ltd.)  in  the  pressure  ranges  of  0–10,000  mbar  at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. S1 in the ESM).
## 2.6    Molecular simulations
The interaction between $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ molecules and one of the PLs models (i.e., P-UiO-66-PL-2) was analyzed from the microcosmic level  via  molecule  simulation  software  Material  Studio  (8.0) package (Accelrys Software Inc). The purpose of the simulation is to confirm the enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selective separation after modified UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ .  Monte  Carlo  (GCMC)  simulations  were  used  to analyze  the  gas  sorption  or  transport  behaviors.  Fractional accessible  volume  (FAV)  for  PL  moles  was  analyzed  using  the “Connolly” surface [43]. A more detailed simulation process can be found in the ESM.
## 3    Results and discussion
## 3.1    Characterizations of $\mathbf{UiO-66-NH}_{2}$
UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ was  firstly  prepared  and  characterized.  First,  the morphology of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ can be observed in the SEM image (Fig. 1(a)), where a regular structure of octahedra can be clearly observed, and the size of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ nanoparticles is ca. $120\mathrm{nm}$ The analysis of TEM image (Fig. 1(b)) is consistent with the SEM image,  where $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanoparticles  reveal  the  same morphology.  In  the  energy  dispersive  X-ray  spectroscopy  (EDS) spectra  of ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2},$ the $_\mathrm{~N~}$ element  is  uniformly  distributed due  to  the  existence  of  2-amino- $^{1,4}$ -benzenedicarboxylic  acid ligand in UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (Fig. S3 in the ESM). At room temperature, the as-prepared UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ is yellow (Fig. 1(c)) by the naked eye identification owing to the existence of amino. The XRD pattern shown in Fig. 1(d) is similar to the simulated and reported ones [42, 44, 45],  further  verifying  the  good  crystal  structure.  Besides, the  adsorption  isotherm  of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ is  nearly  horizontal  at  low  test pressure,  which  belongs  to  type  I  isotherm  and  is  a  typical Langmuir isotherm, indicating that the as-prepared UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ is a kind of microporous material (Fig. 1(e)). Meanwhile, the BET surface  area  of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is  calculated  to  be $897.36~\mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ (Table S1 in the ESM). Besides, the pore size of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ is ca. $0.7\mathrm{nm}$ as reflected in the pore size distribution analysis (Fig. 1(f)), indicating the cavities are micropores, which is consistent with the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption  isotherm.  The  FTIR  test  was  also  carried  out  to analyze  the  pristine $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and  the  peaks  in  the  FTIR spectrum are mainly caused by the 2-amino-1,4- benzenedicarboxylic  acid  ligand  (Fig. $1(\mathrm{g))}$ .  At  the  locations  of 3504 and $3368~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , the two characteristic peaks corresponding to the primary amine group that bonds with the aromatic ring in the ligand  are  attributed  to  asymmetric  and  symmetric  stretching modes  of $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ bonding,  respectively.  The  absorption  peaks  at 1425  and $1573~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could  be  attributed  to  the  C–C  vibrational mode and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-O}$ bonding in the carboxylates. And there are two peaks at 1258 and $1383~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to the bond between aromatic carbon and nitrogen $\mathrm{(C_{ar}-N)}$ [46, 47]. For XPS results of the pristine $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ there are peaks corresponding to O 1s, N 1s, Zr 3p, C 1s, and $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ in the wide scan spectra of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Fig. 1(h)) [48–50], and the high-resolution spectrum of C 1s peaks also verifies the exist of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ , C–N, and C–C bonds (Fig. 1(i)).
## 3.2    Characterizations of the pore generator
By modifying the surface of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ with E-PDMS, the pore generator (P-UiO-66) was obtained and showed a mud-like state rather than powder when they were completely dried (Fig. 2(a)). Then, the pore generator was characterized by various methods. TEM  images  show  that  P-UiO-66  maintains  the  structure  of octahedron but the edges are unclear because of the coating of EPDMS; the neighboring nanoparticles are connected to each other and a polymer layer can be observed on the surface of P-UiO-66 (Figs.  2(b) and $2(\mathrm{c}))$ .  The  XRD  pattern  of  P-UiO-66  is  in  good agreement with the simulated UiO-66 pattern but a little rougher (Fig. 2(d))  due  to  the  coating  of  E-PDMS,  confirming  the crystallinity integrality of P-UiO-66. In the FTIR spectrum of pore generator P-UiO-66 (Fig. 2(e)), there are some new characteristic peaks of E-PDMS compared with that of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . At 1079 and $1010\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ,  there  are  two  stretching  vibration  peaks  derived from the Si–O. The peaks at 864 and $788~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ are attributed to $\mathrm{Si-}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}$ vibrational modes. Considering that E-PDMS contains a  large  amount  of  methyl  groups,  two  characteristic  peaks  of methyl  are  detected  at  2962  and $2906\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}};$ ,  attributing  to  the asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibration of ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ . As for the  ligand,  there  is  one  peak  of  stretching  modes  of $_\mathrm{N-H}$ at $3379~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , corresponding to the secondary amine, which verifies the  reaction  between  the  primary  amine  group  of  2-amino-1,4- benzenedicarboxylic acid and the epoxy group of E-PDMS. And other peaks derived from the ligand are similar to those of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\mathrm{1416.75~cm^{-1}}$ from $\mathrm{C-C,}$ $1573.98\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ from $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-O}$ , and 1257 and $1382~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ from $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{ar}}\mathrm{-}N},$ . Due to the covalent reaction of EPDMS and amino groups of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ the surface of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was coated by E-PDMS and the peaks of $\mathrm{zr}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ in XPS are weak, but the peak of Si is obvious (Figs. $2(\mathrm{f})$ and $2(\mathrm{g),}$ . The thermal  stability  of  P-UiO-66  was  studied  by  TGA  (Fig. $2(\mathrm{h}),$ ). There is no obvious decomposition before $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ indicating the absence of residual solvents (e.g., toluene, DMF, etc.) in P-UiO-66.
## 3.3    Characterizations of porous liquids
The as-prepared porous liquids (P-UiO-66-PLs) were characterized  by  various  methods.  First,  TEM  images  present morphologies of P-UiO-66-PLs (Figs. 3(a)–3(h) and Figs. S4–S11 in  the  ESM).  TEM  images  show  pore  generators  are  connected with  each  other.  And  this  phenomenon  is  more  obvious  with increased sterically hindered solvents (PDMS400 or PDMS6000). The  EDS  images  of  P-UiO-66-PL-4  map  out  the  spatial distribution of C, O, Si, and Zr (Figs. 3(i)–3(l)). Images show that both Si and Zr are uniformly distributed in the particles, indicating a good dispersibility of pore generator in the sterically hindered solvent.
Macroscopic  P-UiO-66-PLs  exhibit  favorable  flow  properties and remain stable after setting for 120 days (Fig. 4(a) and Fig. S12 in  the  ESM),  demonstrating  the  homogeneity,  which  is  of significace in the practical application. Next, the surface groups of P-UiO-66-PLs  were  examined  by  FTIR  spectra  (Figs.  4(b) and 4(c)).  Some  peaks  appear  in  P-UiO-66-PLs  but  not  in  pristine PDMS: $1574~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ from $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-O}$ of the ligand in the ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2},$ 1382 (P-UiO-66-PL-4) and $1386~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ (P-UiO-66-PL-8) from the bond  between  aromatic  carbon  and  nitrogen $\mathrm{(C_{ar}-N)}$ ,  and $3500{-}3300~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ from  the  secondary  amine  of  P-UiO-66,  which indicates the presence of P-UiO-66 in the P-UiO-66-PLs. At the same time, a series of characteristic peaks of PDMS all appear in the  spectra  of  P-UiO-66-PLs.  The  above  FTIR  results demonstrated  the  successful  combination  of  pore  generator  PUiO-66  with  the  sterically  hindered  solvents  (PDMS400  or PDMS6000).
The  thermal  stability  of  P-UiO-66-PLs  was  studied  by  TGA (Figs.  4(d) and 4(e)).  According  to  the  TGA  curves,  the  pure sterically hindered solvent PDMS400 decomposes before $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . For  P-UiO-66-PL-X,  the  first  stage  of  weight  loss  occurs  below $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ due  to  the  presence  of  PDMS400  with  low  molecular weight, and the weight loss proportion in the first stage increases in the order of P-UiO-66-PL-X because the amount of PDMS400 increases  in  the  order  of  P-UiO-66-PL-X.  To  be  specific,  for  PUiO-66-PL-1, there is almost no decomposition before $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ for P-UiO-66-PL-2,  there  is  a  minor  decomposition  of  only  about $10\%$ of weight; for P-UiO-66-PL-3 the decomposition increases to about $25\%$ of  weight;  for  P-UiO-66-PL-4  the  weight  loss  in  the first stage is most excellent, at about $50\%$ . The second weight loss stage occurs at approximately $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . In terms of P-UiO-66-PL-Y, PDMS6000 with high molecular weight is more stable, so all the four PLs started to decompose at about $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
The DSC curves show that the melting points of P-UiO-66-PL$X$ and  P-UiO-66-PL-Y are  all  around $-40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. S13  in  the ESM), providing the first evidence of a room-temperature fluidity of P-UiO-66-PLs. Then, the rheological properties of P-UiO-66- PLs were further tested. In all modulus–temperature curves of PUiO-66-PLs,  the  storage  modulus $\left(G^{\prime}\right)$ is  smaller  than  the  loss modulus $(G^{\prime\prime})$ , indicating liquid properties of P-UiO-66-PLs (Figs. 4(f) and $4(\mathrm{g)}$ and Figs. S14–S16 in the ESM). And in the series of viscosity–temperature  curves  of  P-UiO-66-PLs  based  on PDMS400 or PDMS6000 (Figs. $4(\mathrm{h})$ and 4(i) and Figs. S14–S16 in the  ESM),  as  expected,  for  P-UiO-66-PL-X,  P-UiO-66-PL-4 exhibits the lowest viscosity due to the highest content of sterically hindered solvents, which is $28.97~\mathrm{{mPa}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{S}}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ For the P-UiO66-PL-Y,  P-UiO-66-PL-8  exhibits  the  lowest  viscosity  which  is $542.50~\mathrm{mPa}.\mathrm{S}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ much lower than that of reported in the literature  (Table  S2  in  the  ESM).  The  low  viscosities  can  be attributed to the E-PDMS on the surface of the pore generator PUiO-66, which promotes the stable dispersion of the former in the sterically hindered solvents (PDMS400 or PDMS6000) based on the rule of “like dissolves like”.
Based on the above analysis, constructing type III PLs with low viscosity based on the rule of “like dissolves like” is a smart and meaningful  idea,  while  it  might  be  a  double-edged  sword,  and needs  to  pay  enough  attention  on  the  downside.  The  similarity between the modifying molecules of MOFs and the solvent would benefit the decrease of the viscosity of the PLs, while it would also cause  the  possibility  that  the  cavities  of  the  MOFs  could  be covered  or  blocked  by  both  the  modifying  molecules  and  the solvents, so that the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of the PLs might be limited.  Fortunately,  this  can  be  solved  by  inherent  modular property  of  type  III  PLs  due  to  the  component  adjustability  of porous guest and the sterically hindered solvents. For instance, the viscosities of PLs can be easily regulated by controlling the amount of the sterically hindered solvents. To be specific, by dispersing the pore  generator  P-UiO-66  into  two  classes  of  sterically  hindered solvents  (PDMS400  and  PDMS6000)  with  different  molecular weights according to pre-determined four mass ratios (1:0.5, 1:1, 1:2, and 1:4), P-UiO-66-PL- $X$ and P-UiO-66-PL-Y with different viscosities were obtained. Overall, type III PLs constructed based on the rule of “like dissolves like” expand the gallery of synthesis methods,  and  bring  some  inspiration  to  the  subsequent researchers for preparing PLs with more excellent property.
## 3.4    Gas sorption performance
The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption–desorption performance of P-UiO-66-PLs was tested as shown in Fig. 5. In comparison, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of P-UiO66-PLs  are  all  higher  than  that  of  pristine  sterically  hindered solvents  (PDMS400  or  PDMS6000)  (Figs.  5(a) and 5(b)).  For instance, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of P-UiO-66-PL-2 and P-UiO-66-PL-6 are 39.61 and $23.69~\mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 10 bar, respectively, whereas the values for  PDMS400  and  PDMS6000  are  only  10.07  and $8.79~\mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1};$ , respectively, demonstrating that porous cavities of pore generator P-UiO-66  endow  PLs  with  a  good $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption  performance. Meanwhile, it is worth noting that P-UiO-66-PLs retain most $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ during the desorption process owing to the presence of permanent cavities,  indicating  that  P-UiO-66-PLs  have  the  potential  to  be applied for gas storage. This can be reflected in the hysteresis loop of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption–desorption curves, and all P-UiO-66-PLs exhibit the hysteresis phenomenon, which is consistent with the previous reports  [36, 51, 52].  The  comparison  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption  of  PLs reported in previous studies was also listed in Table S3 in the ESM, and  it  can  be  seen  that  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake  of  P-UiO-66-PLs  is enhanced compared to those in previous literatures.
The interesting thing is that among the series of as-prepared PUiO-66-PLs based on PDMS400 or PDMS6000, for P-UiO-66-PL$X$ series,  P-UiO-66-PL-2  exhibits  the  best $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption performance,  which  is  improved  by $293.52\%$ ,  compared  with pristine sterically hindered solvent PDMS400. For P-UiO-66-PL-Y series, P-UiO-66-PL-6 exhibits the best $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption performance,  which  is  improved  by $169.41\%$ ,  compared  with pristine  sterically  hindered  solvent  PDMS6000  (Figs.  5(c) and $5(\mathrm{d})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ). The reason for the above results is that as $X$ increases from 1 to 2, the increase in sterically hindered solvent promotes the lower viscosity  of  PL.  For  example,  P-UiO-66-PL-2  exhibits  a  lower viscosity  than  P-UiO-66-PL-1.  The  low  viscosity  provides  larger molecular spaces and weaker intermolecular forces, which makes it  easier  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to  access  the  potential  voids  among  the  long organic chains and facilitates improved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption, resulting in higher sorption of P-UiO-66-PL-2 than P-UiO-66-PL-1. Then, as $X$ continues to increase to 3 and 4, although the viscosity of the porous liquid decreases with the increase of the sterically hindered solvent, the concentration of the porous host decreases, resulting in  lower $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption  of  P-UiO-66-PL-3  and  P-UiO-66-PL-4. The mechanism of P-UiO-66-PL-6 is the same as P-UiO-66-PL-2. As a result, P-UiO-PL-2 and P-UiO-66-PL-6 showed better $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption  performance  than  other  porous  liquids  containing corresponding sterically hindered solvents.
In addition, $\Nu_{2}$ sorption–desorption behavior was further tested (Figs.  5(e) and 5(f)).  Although $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptakes  of  P-UiO-66-PLs  are improved  to  different  degrees  compared  with  pristine  sterically hindered solvents (PDMS400 or PDMS6000), the values are much smaller than that of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ indicating the gas separation potential of the as-prepared P-UiO-66-PLs. Therefore, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity was calculated (Figs. $5(\mathrm{g})$ and $5(\mathrm{h})\dot{}\dot{}$ ). Among P-UiO-66-PL- $X,$ , PUiO-66-PL-2 has the best selectivity of 36.21 because of its highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption, and the selectivities of P-UiO-66-PL-3 and P-UiO66-PL-4 are lower than that of PDMS400 due to their both higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ sorption values. Among P-UiO-66-PL-Y, all the four PLs show better $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity than that of PDMS6000, and PUiO-66-PL-6 has the highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 14.27. In the whole P-UiO-66-PLs, P-UiO-66-PL-2 exhibits both optimal $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake $(39.61\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity (36.21) (see Fig. 5(i)).
3.5    The generality of synthesis strategy based on the rule of “like dissolves like”
To verify the generality of the above synthesis strategy based on the  rule  of “like  dissolves  like”,  we  also  used  two  other  aminoMOFs $(\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ as  pore generators to prepare PLs (Figs. S17–S19 in the ESM). For the assynthesized  MIL-53(Al)- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},}\end{array}$ XRD patterns  were  consistent  with  the  simulated  and  reported  ones [53–56], indicating the good crystallinity and topology (Figs. 6(a) and $6(\mathrm{b}))$ .  The  porosity  environment  was  evaluated  by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption experiments. Interestingly, according to $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms  (Figs. $6(\mathrm{c)}$ and $6(\mathrm{d}))$ ,  the  BET  surface  area  of  MIL53(Al)- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was calculated to be ca. $1057.31\ \mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ while the value of MIL-88B(Fe)- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was only $24.48~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Table S4 in the ESM), which  was  due  to  the  pores  shrink  of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ substantially after dehydration, and there is no accessible porosity for $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ at 77 K [57, 58]. SEM and TEM images demonstrate that diamond-shaped  MIL-53(Al)- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystals  and  spindle-shaped $\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe}){\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystals  were  prepared  successfully  (Figs. $6(\mathrm{e})\mathrm{-}6(\mathrm{h})$ and Figs. S20 and S21 in the ESM).
According  to  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption–desorption  behaviors,  for  PUiO-66-PL- $X$ based on the sterically hindered solvent PDMS400, P-UiO-66-PL-2 exhibited the best $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption performance, and for P-UiO-66-PL-Y based on PDMS6000, P-UiO-66-PL-6 showed better sorption performance than the other three PLs. Both these two classes of PLs were prepared based on a 1:1 mass ratio of PUiO-66  to  PDMS400  (or  PDMS6000).  Therefore,  PLs  with  this ratio (denoted by P-MIL-53(Al)-PL- $P$ and P-MIL-88B(Fe)-PL- $Q,$ where $P$ refers to 1 and 2, and $Q$ refers to 1 and 2) were prepared (Fig. S22 in the ESM).
From TEM and EDS images of P-MIL-53(Al)-PL-P and P-MIL88B(Fe)-PL-Q (Figs. $6(\mathrm{i}){-}6(\mathrm{l})$ and Figs. S23–S26 in the ESM), the crystal particles of MIL-53(Al)- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were adhered  to  each  other,  and  it  was  obvious  that  PDMS400  or PDMS6000  was  wrapped  around  the  crystal  surface.  The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption properties of P-MIL-53(Al)-PL-P and P-MIL-88B(Fe)-PL$Q$ were  also  investigated.  As  shown  in Figs.  7(a)–7(d), $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes  of  P-MIL-53(Al)-PL-1,  P-MIL-53(Al)-PL-2,  P-MIL88B(Fe)-PL-1, and P-MIL-88B(Fe)-PL-2 at 10 bar are 36.03, 21.89, 24.57, and $23.26\mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which are all higher than that of pristine sterically hindered solvents (PDMS400 or PDMS6000), showing  that  the  synthesis  strategy  based  on  the  rule  of “like dissolves  like” proposed  in  this  paper  is  general  to  prepare  PLs based on different pore generators.
## 3.6    Molecular simulation and gas uptake mechanism
To  evaluate  the  existence  of  cavities  in  PLs  and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selective sorption  performance,  the  molecular  simulation  was  carried  out (Figs. S27 and S28 in the ESM). The molecular conformation of PUiO-66-PL-2  (here,  it  was  selected  due  to  its  excellent performance for both $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation) after molecular dynamics simulation reveals that the pore generator PUiO-66 successfully prevents the sterically hindered solvent from entering the inner cavity due to the coating of E-PDMS. Besides, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption  density  distribution  was  further  analyzed, illustrating  the  existence  of  cavities.  As  depicted,  the  snapshot shows that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is distributed throughout the P-UiO-66-PL-2 (Fig. 8(a) and Fig. S29  in  the  ESM).  In  addition,  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption density  field  distribution  shows  that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules  are  more concentrated  within  the  pore  generator  compared  with  the sterically  hindered  solvent  (PDMS400),  indicating  that  the  pore generator plays a major role in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selective sorption process. This  also  demonstrates  that  PDMS400  molecular  chains  cannot block  the  pores  of  the  pore  generator.  The $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption  density field distribution shows that only a small number of molecules are distributed  in  PLs  (Fig. 8(b)),  indicating  a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selective separation potential, which is consistent with the results of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptakes. Moreover, the atom volumes field (see the greyblue parts) indicates that most of the fractional accessible volume is attributed to the pore generator and a portion of voids could be attributed  to  the  entanglement  of  PDMS400  molecular  chains (Figs. 8(c) and 8(d)).
According  to  the  results  of  gas  uptake  and  molecular simulation, there are main 3 types of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption sites in the PLs (Fig. 8(e)):
(i) Site 1: the micropores of pore generator P-UiO-66. The $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption  indicates  that $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ had  abundant  micropores, and molecular simulations demonstrate that these pores are not blocked. So these micropores in P-UiO-66 can adsorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by a physical process. In addition, according to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of P-UiO66-PLs, all the as-prepared PLs have a higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption uptake than  the  pure  sterically  hindered  solvent,  which  also  proves  the stable preservation of micropores in P-UiO-66-PLs.
(ii)  Site  2: –NH– in  the  pore  generator  P-UiO-66. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules can be sorbed by $\mathrm{-NH_{-}}$ through chemical interaction. And the low viscosity of P-UiO-66-PLs can provide a convenient environment for the interaction between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and –NH–.
(iii)  Site  3:  potential  voids  among  the  long  organic  chains  of PDMS.  In  this  study,  a  series  of  PLs  with  low  viscosity  were prepared. The low viscosity provides larger molecular spaces and weaker  intermolecular  forces,  which  makes  it  easier  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to access  the  potential  voids  among  the  long  organic  chains. Moreover, there is an interaction between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and the organic chains, which lies in the weak $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption effect derived from the weak sorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by the $-\mathrm{O}-$ via $\mathrm{~C~}(\delta\mathrm{+})...\mathrm{O}\ (\delta\mathrm{-})$ [59–61].  As  for $\mathrm{N}_{2},$ due  to  its  molecular  inertness,  only  a  very small amount is physically sorbed in the PL.
Overall,  combining  the  results  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake  and  molecular simulations, the abundant sorption sites in P-UiO-66-PLs render an excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selective sorption performance. By contrast, $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ is hardly  sorbed  due  to  its  relatively  inert  nature,  which  makes  PUiO-66-PLs great potential for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selective separation.
## 4    Conclusions
In  summary,  we  have  proposed  a  generalizable  and  simple strategy  for  constructing  MOFs-based  PLs  with  low  viscosities. Based on the rule of “like dissolves like”, the pore generator P-UiO66  was  well  dispersed  into  the  sterically  hindered  solvents (PDMS400 or PDMS6000) and a series of type III PLs (P-UiO-66- PL-X and P-UiO-66-PL-Y, where $X$ refers to 1, 2, 3, and 4, and $Y$ refers to 5, 6, 7, and 8) were obtained. As expected, P-UiO-66-PLs have  low  viscosities  at  room  temperature  and  exhibit  a  good fluidity.  For  example,  P-UiO-66-PL-4  exhibited  the  lowest viscosity $28.97~\mathrm{{mPa}.5}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ), which was much lower than the reported ones in other studies. In comparison, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of PUiO-66-PLs  were  all  higher  than  that  of  sterically  hindered solvents (PDMS400 or PDMS6000), indicating that the cavities in P-UiO-66-PLs were well preserved. For instance, compared with the pristine sterically hindered solvents, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of P-UiO-66- PL-2 and P-UiO-66-PL-6 were 39.61 and $23.69~\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 10 bar, which  were  increased  by $293.52\%$ and $169.41\%$ ,  respectively. Besides, the low $\Nu_{2}$ uptakes of P-UiO-66-PLs implied the potential for  gas  separation,  and  P-UiO-66-PL-2  exhibited  the  highest selectivity  of  36.21.  Moreover,  this  was  also  confirmed  by  the molecular simulations, revealing a denser $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ distribution density than  that  of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ in  PLs,  which  is  attributed  to  the  abundant sorption sites of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in P-UiO-66-PLs. Besides, the generality of the synthesis strategy based on the rule of “like dissolves like” was confirmed  by  the  successful  preparation  of  other  four  classes  of PLs  (P-MIL-53(Al)-PL- $P$ and  P-MIL-88B(Fe)-PL-Q)  using  two other amino-MOFs. Taking account into the diversity of advanced porous  materials  (APMs,  e.g.,  MOFs,  MOPs,  COFs,  etc.)  and sterically hindered solvents (e.g., ionic liquids or oligomer species), the  developed  generalizable  strategy  provides  new  opportunities for the design and construction of APMs-based PLs that are closer to the practical applications of gas capture, storage, and separation, etc.
